-- | OpenZeppelin AccessControl — a standalone, token-agnostic role-based
-- access-control substrate.
--
-- The Daml analogue of `AccessControl.sol`: an authority (`admin`, the
-- `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` holder) grants named roles to accounts, and gated
-- operations require that the caller holds the right role. Independent — no
-- dependency on the Ownable or Pausable packages — so a project that wants only
-- RBAC imports only this DAR.
--
-- # Roles are Text ids (the `bytes32` analogue), on purpose
-- Solidity identifies roles with `bytes32` constants (e.g.
-- `keccak256("MINTER_ROLE")`). A *generic* Daml library must do something
-- equivalent, because **Daml templates are monomorphic**: a template field
-- cannot be a type variable, so there is no `template RoleGrant r with role : r`.
-- A reusable role primitive therefore stores `role : Text`. This is the
-- deliberate genericity/type-safety trade documented in the canton-token-template
-- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`: the library is generic (Text ids, reusable by any
-- contract) and a consumer that wants compile-time exhaustiveness layers its own
-- closed role sum on top via a thin `roleId : MyRole -> Text` wrapper (see the
-- test package for a worked example).
--
-- # Keyless model (Daml-LF 2.1)
-- No contract keys, so there is no global role map to query. A `RoleGrant` is a
-- bearer credential, signed by `admin` and naming one `account`. Possession of a
-- valid grant *is* the authorization: a gated choice fetches the grant the
-- caller presents (by `ContractId`, disclosed where needed) and `requireRole`
-- checks it is admin-signed, names the caller, and carries the required role.
-- Soundness without keys: the grant is `admin`-signed (unforgeable) and names a
-- specific `account`, so the worst an adversary can do is present a grant they
-- do not hold and fail their own authorization.
module OpenZeppelin.AccessControl where

-- | A role granted by `admin` to `account`. Possession is authorization.
--
-- Signatories: `admin` (the role authority; the grant is unforgeable).
-- Observers: `account` (so the grantee can see and present its own credential).
-- Controllers: `account` controls `RoleGrant_Renounce` (self-renunciation);
--   grants are otherwise minted/revoked through `RoleAdmin` and validated by the
--   pure `requireRole`/`hasRole` functions.
-- Choices: `RoleGrant_Renounce` (the grantee gives up its own role; the
--   `renounceRole` analogue).
-- Disclosed parties: `admin` and `account` are stakeholders; a gated operation
--   the caller invokes is given the grant by disclosure when the operation's
--   signatory is not already a stakeholder.
-- Privacy expectations: only `admin` and `account` (and disclosed verifiers)
--   learn of the grant.
-- Authorization assumptions: only `admin` can create a grant (via `RoleAdmin`),
--   so role membership cannot be forged. Only `account` can renounce its own
--   grant — `RoleGrant_Renounce` is self-only by construction (the controller is
--   the grantee), mirroring Solidity's `renounceRole(role, callerConfirmation)`
--   self-check.
-- Archival behavior: revoked via `RoleAdmin_RevokeRole`/`RoleAdmin_RevokeRoleAs`
--   (admin-authorized archive) or self-archived via `RoleGrant_Renounce`.
-- Failure modes: presenting another account's grant fails `requireRole`'s
--   assignee check; a wrong-admin or wrong-role grant fails its respective
--   check.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: 0.x, unstable; not yet public API.
template RoleGrant
  with
    admin : Party
      -- ^ The role authority (DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE analogue) that issued this grant.
    account : Party
      -- ^ The party granted the role.
    role : Text
      -- ^ The role identifier, e.g. "MINTER_ROLE".
  where
    signatory admin
    observer account

    -- | The grantee renounces its own role, archiving this grant. Self-only by
    -- construction: `account` is the controller, so a party can only ever
    -- renounce a grant that names it (the `renounceRole` analogue). The archive
    -- is authorized by `admin`'s signatory authority carried by the contract, so
    -- the grantee needs no admin key.
    choice RoleGrant_Renounce : ()
      controller account
      do pure ()

-- | The role-administration authority: mints and revokes `RoleGrant`s.
--
-- Two issuance/revocation paths coexist:
--   * The root path (`RoleAdmin_GrantRole`/`RoleAdmin_RevokeRole`, controlled by
--     `admin`) — the DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE holder manages any role directly.
--   * The role-admin path (`RoleAdmin_GrantRoleAs`/`RoleAdmin_RevokeRoleAs`,
--     controlled by an arbitrary `caller`) — the `getRoleAdmin` analogue. The
--     caller presents a grant for a supplied `adminRole : Text` and may then
--     grant/revoke the target `role`. The new grant is `admin`-signed because the
--     choice runs with this contract's authority, so a delegate administers roles
--     without holding the admin key.
--
-- Mechanism-only and generic: the library hard-codes **no** role→admin graph.
-- `adminRole` is a caller-supplied `Text`; a consumer that wants a fixed
-- hierarchy computes which admin role gates which target role in its own code
-- (e.g. a compiled `roleAdmin : Role -> Role` mapped through `roleId`) and passes
-- the result here. This preserves the `bytes32`-style Text genericity end-to-end.
--
-- Signatories: `admin`. Observers: none.
-- Controllers: `admin` for the root grant/revoke and begin-transfer choices; an
--   arbitrary `caller` for the role-admin grant/revoke choices.
-- Choices: `RoleAdmin_GrantRole`, `RoleAdmin_RevokeRole`, `RoleAdmin_GrantRoleAs`,
--   `RoleAdmin_RevokeRoleAs`, `RoleAdmin_BeginDefaultAdminTransfer`.
-- Disclosed parties: `admin`; the `RoleAdmin` is disclosed to a `caller` of the
--   role-admin choices (the consumer's off-ledger service provides this).
-- Privacy expectations: the admin contract is otherwise private to `admin`.
-- Authorization assumptions: only `admin` administers roles directly; a delegate
--   may administer a role only by presenting a valid grant for that role's
--   admin role. The DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE itself is **not** special-cased here — any
--   restriction on re-delegating root (the `AccessControlDefaultAdminRules`
--   posture) is the consumer's policy, layered on top.
-- Archival behavior: all choices are nonconsuming; the `RoleAdmin` persists.
-- Failure modes: the revoke choices reject a grant minted by a different admin
--   (eRoleAdminMismatch); the role-admin choices fail `requireRole` if `caller`
--   does not hold a valid `adminRole` grant; begin-transfer rejects a self-offer
--   (eDefaultAdminSameParty).
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: 0.x, unstable; not yet public API.
template RoleAdmin
  with
    admin : Party
      -- ^ The DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE holder.
  where
    signatory admin

    -- | Grant `role` to `account`. Returns the new grant's id.
    nonconsuming choice RoleAdmin_GrantRole : ContractId RoleGrant
      with
        account : Party
        role : Text
      controller admin
      do
        create RoleGrant with admin; account; role

    -- | Revoke a grant this admin issued.
    nonconsuming choice RoleAdmin_RevokeRole : ()
      with
        grantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
      controller admin
      do
        grant <- fetch grantCid
        assertMsg eRoleAdminMismatch (grant.admin == admin)
        archive grantCid

    -- | Role-admin-aware grant (`getRoleAdmin` analogue): `caller` grants `role`
    -- to `account` by presenting its own grant for `adminRole` — the role that
    -- administers `role`. The library imposes no role graph; the consumer decides
    -- which `adminRole` gates `role` and supplies it. The new `RoleGrant` is
    -- `admin`-signed (created with this contract's authority).
    nonconsuming choice RoleAdmin_GrantRoleAs : ContractId RoleGrant
      with
        caller : Party
          -- ^ The delegate exercising the grant.
        adminRole : Text
          -- ^ The role that administers `role` (consumer-supplied).
        adminGrantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
          -- ^ The caller's grant for `adminRole`.
        account : Party
          -- ^ Party to receive `role`.
        role : Text
          -- ^ Role to grant.
      controller caller
      do
        adminGrant <- fetch adminGrantCid
        requireRole caller adminRole admin adminGrant
        create RoleGrant with admin; account; role

    -- | Role-admin-aware revoke: `caller` revokes `grantCid` by presenting its
    -- own grant for `adminRole`. The revoked grant must have been issued by this
    -- registry's `admin`.
    nonconsuming choice RoleAdmin_RevokeRoleAs : ()
      with
        caller : Party
          -- ^ The delegate exercising the revoke.
        adminRole : Text
          -- ^ The role that administers the revoked grant's role.
        adminGrantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
          -- ^ The caller's grant for `adminRole`.
        grantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
          -- ^ The grant to revoke.
      controller caller
      do
        adminGrant <- fetch adminGrantCid
        requireRole caller adminRole admin adminGrant
        grant <- fetch grantCid
        assertMsg eRoleAdminMismatch (grant.admin == admin)
        archive grantCid

    -- | Begin a two-step, timelocked transfer of a role to `newAdmin` — the
    -- `AccessControlDefaultAdminRules.beginDefaultAdminTransfer` analogue. The
    -- nominee cannot accept before `effectiveTime`, and `admin` can cancel within
    -- the window. Pass the DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE id as `role` for a default-admin
    -- handoff; the mechanism is generic over any role.
    nonconsuming choice RoleAdmin_BeginDefaultAdminTransfer : ContractId DefaultAdminTransferOffer
      with
        newAdmin : Party
          -- ^ The nominee who must accept after the timelock.
        role : Text
          -- ^ The role id to grant on acceptance (e.g. DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE).
        effectiveTime : Time
          -- ^ Earliest ledger time the nominee may accept.
      controller admin
      do
        assertMsg eDefaultAdminSameParty (newAdmin /= admin)
        create DefaultAdminTransferOffer with admin; newAdmin; role; effectiveTime

-- | A pending, timelocked transfer of a role to `newAdmin` — the
-- `AccessControlDefaultAdminRules` analogue. It is the oz-ownable two-step
-- handshake shape (offer → accept, with a cancel) plus a ledger-time gate:
-- `newAdmin` cannot accept before `effectiveTime`. Acceptance grants `role` by
-- creating a `RoleGrant` for `newAdmin`.
--
-- Self-contained on purpose: this package deliberately carries **no dependency**
-- on oz-ownable (the three library packages stay independent, see
-- canton-token-template docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3/§8), so it re-expresses the
-- handshake rather than importing `OwnershipOffer` — exactly as Solidity's
-- `AccessControlDefaultAdminRules` reimplements its own two-step instead of
-- inheriting `Ownable2Step`. The time gate is what `AccessControlDefaultAdminRules`
-- adds over a plain two-step (`defaultAdminDelay`): it prevents a rushed or
-- coerced handoff and leaves a cancellation window.
--
-- Mechanism-only and generic: it grants whatever `role` the consumer supplies
-- and hard-codes no role graph. It does **not** revoke any prior holder's grant
-- (a role can have several holders); pair it with a revoke if the consumer wants
-- exclusive transfer semantics.
--
-- Signatories: `admin` (the authority initiating the handoff).
-- Observers: `newAdmin` (must see the offer to accept it).
-- Controllers: `newAdmin` accepts; `admin` cancels.
-- Choices: `DefaultAdminTransferOffer_Accept`, `DefaultAdminTransferOffer_Cancel`.
-- Disclosed parties: `admin` and `newAdmin` are stakeholders.
-- Privacy expectations: limited to the two parties to the handshake.
-- Authorization assumptions: only `newAdmin` may accept (so the role never lands
--   on a party that has not actively claimed it); only `admin` may cancel.
-- Archival behavior: both choices are consuming; accept creates the new
--   `RoleGrant`, cancel creates nothing.
-- Failure modes: accepting before `effectiveTime` fails
--   (eDefaultAdminTimelockNotElapsed).
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: 0.x, unstable; not yet public API.
template DefaultAdminTransferOffer
  with
    admin : Party
      -- ^ The role authority initiating the handoff.
    newAdmin : Party
      -- ^ The nominee who must accept after the timelock.
    role : Text
      -- ^ The role id to grant on acceptance (e.g. DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE).
    effectiveTime : Time
      -- ^ Earliest ledger time the nominee may accept.
  where
    signatory admin
    observer newAdmin

    -- | Accept the handoff once the timelock has elapsed; grants `role` to
    -- `newAdmin`. The new grant is `admin`-signed (this contract's authority).
    choice DefaultAdminTransferOffer_Accept : ContractId RoleGrant
      controller newAdmin
      do
        requireTimelockElapsed effectiveTime
        create RoleGrant with admin; account = newAdmin; role

    -- | Cancel the pending handoff before it is accepted (the
    -- `cancelDefaultAdminTransfer` analogue).
    choice DefaultAdminTransferOffer_Cancel : ()
      controller admin
      do pure ()

-- | Assert that the current ledger time has reached `effectiveTime` — the
-- timelock gate shared by `DefaultAdminTransferOffer` and reusable by consumers
-- that run their own timelocked-handoff template over a richer credential.
requireTimelockElapsed : Time -> Update ()
requireTimelockElapsed effectiveTime = do
  now <- getTime
  assertMsg eDefaultAdminTimelockNotElapsed (now >= effectiveTime)

-- | Predicate: does `grant` authorize `caller` for `role` under `admin`?
-- Pure form, for callers that already hold the fetched grant.
hasRole : Party -> Text -> Party -> RoleGrant -> Bool
hasRole caller role admin grant =
  grant.admin == admin && grant.account == caller && grant.role == role

-- | Assert that `caller` holds `role` under `admin`, given the grant they
-- present. The `requireRole` analogue — call it at the top of a gated choice.
--
-- Checks (mirroring daml-verify A1-A3):
--   * grant.admin == admin    — credential issued by this registry's authority
--   * grant.account == caller — anti-impersonation (caller names itself)
--   * grant.role == role      — the credential carries the required role
requireRole : Party -> Text -> Party -> RoleGrant -> Update ()
requireRole caller role admin grant = do
  assertMsg eRoleAdminMismatch (grant.admin == admin)
  assertMsg eRoleAssigneeMismatch (grant.account == caller)
  assertMsg eRoleMismatch (grant.role == role)

-- Error messages (inlined to keep this package dependency-free).

eRoleAdminMismatch : Text
eRoleAdminMismatch = "AccessControl: grant admin is not the expected authority"

eRoleAssigneeMismatch : Text
eRoleAssigneeMismatch = "AccessControl: grant does not name the caller (impersonation)"

eRoleMismatch : Text
eRoleMismatch = "AccessControl: grant does not carry the required role"

eDefaultAdminSameParty : Text
eDefaultAdminSameParty = "AccessControl: default-admin handoff nominee is the current admin"

eDefaultAdminTimelockNotElapsed : Text
eDefaultAdminTimelockNotElapsed = "AccessControl: default-admin transfer timelock has not elapsed"
